lessons.content:
Explanation: A component describes a piece of interface. JSX combines markup-like syntax with JavaScript expressions, while component names begin with a capital letter.

Example: function CourseTitle() {
  return <h2>Interface Foundations</h2>;
}

Exercise: Create a Welcome component that returns a heading and a short paragraph inside one parent element.

Explanation: Props are inputs supplied by a parent. Treat them as read-only and keep the same component useful with different data.

Example: function LessonLabel({ title, position }) {
  return <span>{position}. {title}</span>;
}
// Usage: <LessonLabel title="Components" position={1} />

Exercise: Create a CourseSummary component accepting title and instructorName. Render it twice with different props.

Explanation: Render arrays by mapping records to elements. A stable record identifier helps React preserve the correct item when the list changes.

Example: function LessonList({ lessons }) {
  return <ul>{lessons.map(lesson => (
    <li key={lesson.id}>{lesson.title}</li>
  ))}</ul>;
}

Exercise: Render three lessons with stable IDs. Explain why an array index can identify the wrong item after reordering.

Explanation: State stores information that affects rendering. An event handler requests an update; a functional update safely derives the next value from the previous value.

Example: function LessonCounter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <button onClick={() => setCount(value => value + 1)}>{count}</button>;
}
// Import useState from React.

Exercise: Build a button that toggles a details panel. Use a boolean state value rather than changing the DOM manually.

Explanation: Place shared state in the nearest common parent. Pass values downward and callbacks upward so several components use one source of truth.

Example: function SearchPage() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');
  return <><SearchField value={query} onChange={setQuery} /><Results query={query} /></>;
}

Exercise: Sketch a parent, filter control, and results list. Identify which component should own the selected category.

Explanation: Effects synchronize a component with an external system after rendering. Return cleanup for subscriptions so unmounting or changed dependencies do not leave stale listeners.

Example: useEffect(() => {
  const onResize = () => setWidth(window.innerWidth);
  window.addEventListener('resize', onResize);
  return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', onResize);
}, []);
// Import useEffect and initialize width state in the component.

Exercise: Describe the cleanup needed for a timer. Explain why computing a filtered array usually does not require an effect.

Explanation: A request-driven screen needs distinct loading, error, empty, and successful states. Keep each message truthful rather than showing a stale count as current.

Example: if (isPending) return <p role="status">Loading courses...</p>;
if (error) return <button onClick={retry}>Retry loading</button>;
if (courses.length === 0) return <p>No courses yet.</p>;
return <CourseList courses={courses} />;

Exercise: List the four states for a lesson request and write a useful user-facing message for each.

Explanation: An index can speed selected lookups at the cost of storage and write work. Use EXPLAIN to inspect the planned access path instead of assuming every column needs an index.

Example: EXPLAIN SELECT id, title FROM courses WHERE slug = 'data-basics';

Exercise: Name a lookup that benefits from a unique slug index and one reason not to index every text field.

Explanation: A route combines a URL with an HTTP method. Match the method to the operation and make the response contract clear.

Example: from fastapi import FastAPI
app = FastAPI()

@app.get('/health')
def health():
    return {'status': 'ok'}

Exercise: Describe which methods you would use to list courses, create a course, and partially edit its title.

Explanation: A response model defines the safe output shape. Avoid returning internal ORM fields simply because they happen to be available.

Example: class CourseSummary(BaseModel):
    title: str
    slug: str

@app.get('/example', response_model=CourseSummary)
def example():
    return {'title': 'API Basics', 'slug': 'api-basics'}

Exercise: List fields a public user response should include and fields it must never expose.

Explanation: Use const for bindings you do not reassign and let when reassignment is required. Prefer descriptive names; const does not make an object immutable.

Example: const course = { title: 'Web Basics' };
course.title = 'Web Foundations';
let completed = 0;
completed += 1;

Exercise: Explain why changing course.title is allowed even though course was declared with const.

Explanation: Conditions choose a branch. Strict equality compares without type coercion, making differences between text and numbers explicit.

Example: const completed = 3;
const total = 6;
if (completed === total) {
  console.log('Complete');
} else {
  console.log('Keep going');
}

Exercise: Compare 3 === '3' and 3 === 3. Write a condition for a non-empty course title.

Explanation: Functions package a transformation or action. Return values let the caller decide how to use the result.

Example: function progressPercentage(completed, total) {
  return total === 0 ? 0 : (completed / total) * 100;
}

Exercise: Write a function returning a learner's display label from their name and completed lesson count.

Explanation: Array methods can create new collections without editing the original. Use map to transform entries and filter to choose entries.

Example: const courses = [{ title: 'Web', level: 'beginner' }, { title: 'APIs', level: 'intermediate' }];
const titles = courses.filter(course => course.level === 'beginner').map(course => course.title);

Exercise: Filter a list of lessons to completed entries, then produce a list containing only their titles.

Explanation: Objects group related properties. Optional chaining reads through a possibly absent value without treating missing data as a successful result.

Example: const instructor = { profile: { fullName: 'Mira Chen' } };
const label = instructor.profile?.fullName ?? 'Instructor unavailable';

Exercise: Build a course object with an optional category. Display a useful fallback when the category is absent.

Explanation: Browser events connect user actions to behavior. Use native controls and update text with textContent rather than inserting untrusted markup.

Example: const button = document.querySelector('button');
const status = document.querySelector('[role="status"]');
button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  status.textContent = 'Ready to learn';
});
// The page must contain both elements.

Exercise: Create a button and status paragraph. Update the paragraph when the button is activated with a mouse or keyboard.

Explanation: An element's box includes content, padding, border, and margin. border-box makes declared width include padding and border, simplifying sizing.

Example: * { box-sizing: border-box; }
.lesson-card { width: 100%; max-width: 32rem; padding: 1rem; border: 1px solid; }

Exercise: Draw a card box and label its content, padding, border, and margin. Explain what max-width changes.

Explanation: Responsive layouts adapt to available space instead of assuming a single device width. Let content wrap and test narrow viewports for overflow.

Example: .courses { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1rem; }
@media (min-width: 48rem) { .courses { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); } }

Exercise: Describe how a two-column course grid should behave at 320px. Identify one long-text overflow risk.

Explanation: A browser sends HTTP requests and receives responses containing status, headers, and sometimes a body. Handle unsuccessful responses explicitly.

Example: const response = await fetch('/api/v1/health');
if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Health check unavailable');
const result = await response.json();
// A 204 response has no JSON body and must be handled separately.

Exercise: Describe what the interface should show while a request is loading, after an error, and after an empty successful result.
